    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2023886388224045091",
      "url": "https://x.com/Stefano_1799/status/2023886388224045091",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Stefano_1799/status/2023886388224045091",
      "text": "After a deep scrape of every #DFC #BoardofDirectors agenda from 2020 to today, $GLO is an outlier:\n\n- 28 meetings identified\n- 108 projects listed for approval (3,86 projects per meeting)\n- only 6 (?) publicly listed counterparties across the full sample\n\n-> None clearly tied to major Western exchanges in the usual sense (North America / Western Europe), with listings instead appearing in markets such as Istanbul, Palestine, the Philippines, etc.\n\nAgainst that backdrop, #GlobalAtomic appears to be a near outlier: a company publicly traded on Western markets (#Canada/#Europe), also accessible to U.S. investors, reaching the DFC approval pipeline.\nA second point is just as notable: almost every meeting includes a project-approval list, except June 2025 (1 out of 28). \n\nThe last 20th February 2026 meeting is associated with a specific Sunshine Act exemption, which reasonably raises additional questions and warrants closer scrutiny.\nIf all goes well, I’ll publish a full breakdown with data, sources, and methodology.\n\n#uranium #U308 #nuclear #dasa $GLO #GLO #Niger",
