      "quoted_tweet": {
        "type": "tweet",
        "id": "2022743855175516162",
        "url": "https://x.com/Stefano_1799/status/2022743855175516162",
        "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Stefano_1799/status/2022743855175516162",
        "text": "Here’s a snapshot of the #DFC – #GlobalAtomic situation 🧩\n\nIf every DFC update or every agenda publication (especially the one released yesterday) makes you panic because the situation doesn’t feel perfectly clear, this is for you 🤝\n\n1) Where things stand 📌\nCredit Committee approval ⏩ (and it was disclosed to the market)\nInvestment Committee (IC) is the next materially relevant step 🔍\nDFC does not publish intermediate committee outcomes to the public 🚫\n\n2) How communication actually works 🗣️\n\nIn general, the bank does not provide notifications about delays ⏳\nThe bank tends to communicate to the company only once steps are completed ✅ rather than providing incremental updates while the process is still ongoing.\nSo visibility between milestones is often limited 🔦\n\n3) Board of Directors – February 20 meeting 🏛️ (the “weird” part)\n\nThe agenda published for February 20 looks unusual compared to the historical pattern 👇\nSince 2021, all Board meeting agendas included a “Project Approvals” section listing projects, with the only exception of June 2025.\nFor the “same” January meeting window, an initial agenda listed two projects for approval.\nThe February 20 agenda includes none, which is objectively unusual 🚨\n➡️ This is an objective data point that makes this agenda anomalous.\n\n4) What this could imply (without predicting anything) 🧠\n\nThe agenda could be updated closer to the date 📝\nThis may be a meeting convened mainly for other items (e.g., audit committee report) 📄\nA separate meeting may follow later where project approvals are addressed 🔑\nGlobal may not appear on the public agenda because it is a publicly traded company. In that case, we would only find out through a company press release.\n\n5) Timing and the “visibility problem” ⌛\n\nDFC moves slower than markets would like 🐢 and operates differently from a standard commercial lender.\nIt’s also not used to working with publicly traded companies 📈, where disclosure expectations and timing sensitivity are much higher.\n\nBottom line 🎯\nAs of today:\nNo public confirmation of Investment Committee approval ❔\nNo project approvals listed in the Feb 20 agenda 📭\nBetween milestones, visibility is structurally limited — so the clean signals are completed steps ✅ and, ultimately, Board-level approvals 🏁\nWe track the documents 🧾. \nWe stick to what’s observable 👀. \nWe avoid filling gaps with assumptions 🧊.\n\n#uranium #GLO $GLO #U308 #dasa #niger #sahel #financing #URA",
